   109	## 4. The Grid CO Step 1 finding
   110	
   111	Task 7 tested the design's E4 (permanent exclude) classification
   112	against a Grid CO variant, `gridCOx`, whose only change from `gridCO`
   113	is its `excludes` function. Step 1 asked whether the
   114	equivocation-exclusion disjunct could be dropped from `excludes`: kept
   115	only if some clock evaluation removes a vote, dropped otherwise. The
   116	proof (`clock_votes`, `AvailableChainRegistry/GridCOHooks.lean`)
   117	established that no clock evaluation — `expire`, `freeze`, the early
   118	save, `release`, or `gate` — writes `st.votes`. Only `admitVote`
   119	(called from `process`, never from `clock`) appends to it. Every clock
   120	step reads or replays existing votes; none removes one.
   121	
   122	The finding keeps the equivocation-exclusion disjunct in
   123	`gridExcludes`: an already-equivocating seat stays excluded until the
   124	epoch ends, because nothing in `clock` would ever revoke that
   125	exclusion. `Deliverable`, `RelayFeasible`, and `WindowMargin` for Grid
   126	CO move to Phase 1, with the `excludes` change in `gridCO` itself
   127	(deviation (b)).
